{"cuisine/cuisine-of-new-jersey":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/cuisine-of-new-jersey","n":"Cuisine of New Jersey","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Cuisine of the Mid-Atlantic United States","cuisine/cuisine-of-the-mid-atlantic-united-states"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in United States of America","intro":[["The cuisine of New Jersey is derived from the state's long immigrant history and its close proximity to both New York City and Philadelphia. Due to its geographical location, New Jersey can generally be divided by New York City cuisine in the northern and central parts of the state and Philadelphia cuisine in the southern parts. Restaurants in the state often make use of locally grown ingredients such as asparagus, blueberries, cranberries, tomatoes, corn, and peaches. New Jersey is particularly known for its diners, of which there are approximately 525, the most of any state. Various foods invented in the state, such as the pork roll, and salt water taffy, remain popular there today."],["The state is known for its commercial food and industrial production, beginning with the founding of Campbell's, headquartered in Camden since 1869. Goya Foods, the largest Hispanic-owned food company in the United States, operates a state-of-the-art, sustainable corporate headquarters in Jersey City. M&M's candy has been produced in Hackettstown, New Jersey since 1958. Manischewitz, one of the top producers of Jewish food such as matzo and kosher wine, is headquartered in Newark, New Jersey."],["Food writer, influencer, and chef Anthony Bourdain was raised in Leonia, New Jersey. He often profiled New Jersey restaurants on his multiple television shows. Carlo's Bake Shop in Hoboken is the setting for TLC's reality television series, Cake Boss. New Jersey is also well known for having convenience food stores, such as Wawa and QuickChek, along with submarine sandwich restaurants, like Jersey Mike's Subs."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"cuisine-of-new-jersey"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuisine_of_New_Jersey"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Cuisine of the Mid-Atlantic United States","cuisine/cuisine-of-the-mid-atlantic-united-states"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Dishing Up New Jersey: 150 Recipes from the Garden State","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"John Holl","note":"New Jersey recipes from diners, farms, and shore towns.","url":"https://specialsales.hbgusa.com/titles/john-holl/dishing-up%c2%ae-new-jersey/9781612126661","suggested":true,"isbn":"9781612126661"},{"name":"The Jersey Shore Cookbook: Fresh Summer Flavors from the Boardwalk and Beyond","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Deborah Smith","note":"Summer recipes from Jersey Shore restaurants and boardwalks.","url":"https://www.quirkbooks.com/9781594748721/the-jersey-shore-cookbook/","suggested":true,"isbn":"9781594748721"}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Cuisine of the Mid-Atlantic United States","cuisine/cuisine-of-the-mid-atlantic-united-states"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas","img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/f/f0/Taylor_pork_roll_slices_on_pkg.JPG/500px-Taylor_pork_roll_slices_on_pkg.JPG","w":500,"h":419,"by":"Austin Murphy (talk)","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Taylor_pork_roll_slices_on_pkg.JPG"}},"cuisine/kosovan-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/kosovan-cuisine","n":"Kosovan cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Albanian cuisine","cuisine/cuisine-of-albania"]],"sub":"National cuisine","intro":[["The cuisine of Kosovo is a representative of the cuisine of the Balkans and consists of traditional dishes by ethnic groups native to Kosovo. Due to Albanians being the main ethnic group in Kosovo, it is mainly an expression of Albanian cuisine, also adopting some elements of other Balkan countries."],["Bread, dairy, meat, fruits and vegetables are important staples in Kosovan cuisine. With diversity of recipes, the Kosovan daily cuisine adjusts well to the country's occasional hot summers and the frequent long winters. As a result of its continental climate, fresh vegetables are consumed in summer while pickles throughout autumn and winter."],["Breakfast in Kosovo is usually light, consisting primarily of a croissant with coffee, sandwiches, scrambled eggs, omelettes, petulla or toast with salami, processed cheese, lettuce and tea. Cereals with milk, waffles, pretzels and homemade pancakes with honey or marmalade are also frequently consumed especially by children."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"kosovan-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kosovan_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Balkan Lunchbox: Kosovo recipes","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"balkanlunchbox.com","note":"Balkan home cooking blog with a section of Kosovar dishes.","url":"https://balkanlunchbox.com/category/country/kosovo-recipes","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Albanian cuisine","cuisine/cuisine-of-albania"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine","img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/b/b6/Mmm%2C_clay_pot_and_pickled_vegetables.jpg/500px-Mmm%2C_clay_pot_and_pickled_vegetables.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Erica _ Fischer","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mmm%2C_clay_pot_and_pickled_vegetables.jpg"}},"dish/achma":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/achma","n":"Achma","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Georgian cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Achma is a traditional Georgian dish and a variety of khachapuri from region of Adjara."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-achma"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Achma"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Georgian cuisine","cuisine/georgian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":3,"links":[["Khachapuri","ingredient/khachapuri"],["Sulguni","ingredient/sulguni"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Georgian Recipes","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"georgianrecipes.net","note":"Recipe site dedicated to traditional Georgian dishes.","url":"https://georgianrecipes.net/about-georgian-recipes","suggested":true,"kind":"site"}]},{"h":"More from Georgian cuisine","links":[["Ajika","dish/ajika"],["Akhasheni","dish/akhasheni"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Chakapuli","dish/chakapuli"],["Chakhokhbili","dish/chakhokhbili"],["Chashushuli","dish/chashushuli"],["Chicken tabaka","dish/chicken-tabaka"],["Churchkhela","dish/churchkhela"],["Dambalkhacho","dish/dambalkhacho"],["Eggplant jam","dish/eggplant-jam"],["Gozinaki","dish/gozinaki"],["Khachapuri","dish/khachapuri"],["Kharcho","dish/kharcho"],["Khash (dish)","dish/khash-dish"],["Khvanchkara","dish/khvanchkara"],["Kindzmarauli","dish/kindzmarauli"],["Kubdari","dish/kubdari"],["Kuchmachi","dish/kuchmachi"],["Kvass","dish/kvass"],["Lobiani","dish/lobiani"],["Mchadi","dish/mchadi"],["Mujuretuli","dish/mujuretuli"],["Nadugi","dish/nadugi"],["Nigvziani badrijani","dish/nigvziani-badrijani"],["Pkhali","dish/pkhali"],["Shkmeruli","dish/shkmeruli"],["Tsolikouri","dish/tsolikouri"],["Tvishi (wine)","dish/tvishi-wine"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/b/b4/%D0%9F%D0%B8%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B3_%D0%90%D1%87%D0%BC%D0%B0_01.jpg/500px-%D0%9F%D0%B8%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B3_%D0%90%D1%87%D0%BC%D0%B0_01.jpg","w":500,"h":376,"by":"Pannet","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%9F%D0%B8%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B3_%D0%90%D1%87%D0%BC%D0%B0_01.jpg"}},"ingredient/maize-flour":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/maize-flour","n":"Maize flour","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 5 dishes across 7 cuisines","intro":[["Maize flour or corn flour is a flour ground from dried maize (corn). It is a common staple food, and is ground to coarse, medium, and fine consistencies. Coarsely ground corn flour (meal) is known as cornmeal. When maize flour is made from maize that has been soaked in an alkaline solution, e.g., limewater (a process known as nixtamalization), it is called masa harina (or masa flour), which is used for making tamales and tortillas. Arepas are typically made from corn flour that has not been nixtamalized."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-maize-flour"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maize_flour"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":7,"groups":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine",[["Grits","dish/grits"]]],["Colombian cuisine","cuisine/colombian-cuisine",[["Arepa","dish/arepa"]]],["Georgian cuisine","cuisine/georgian-cuisine",[["Mchadi","dish/mchadi"]]],["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine",[["Grits","dish/grits"]]],["Paraguayan cuisine","cuisine/paraguayan-cuisine",[["Sopa paraguaya","dish/sopa-paraguaya"]]],["Ukrainian cuisine","cuisine/ukrainian-cuisine",[["Banosh","dish/banosh"]]],["Venezuelan cuisine","cuisine/venezuelan-cuisine",[["Arepa","dish/arepa"]]]]},{"h":"Where it's grown","grown":{"what":"Maize (corn)","years":"2020 to 2024","url":"https://www.fao.org/faostat/en/#data/QCL","world":1194842295,"top":[["United States of America","cuisine/american-cuisine",370796660],["China","cuisine/chinese-cuisine",278837460],["Brazil","cuisine/brazilian-cuisine",109781604],["Argentina","cuisine/argentine-cuisine",55372549],["India","cuisine/indian-cuisine",35054811],["Ukraine","cuisine/ukrainian-cuisine",31296260],["Mexico","cuisine/mexican-cuisine",26671534],["South Africa","cuisine/south-african-cuisine",15381709],["Russia","cuisine/russian-cuisine",14786645],["Canada","cuisine/canadian-cuisine",14695763],["Indonesia","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",14556896],["France","cuisine/french-cuisine",13493422],["Nigeria","cuisine/nigerian-cuisine",12062734],["Ethiopia","cuisine/ethiopian-cuisine",11550100],["Pakistan","cuisine/pakistani-cuisine",9677670]]}},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Cornmeal","ingredient/cornmeal"],["Maize meal","ingredient/maize-meal"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Bryndza","ingredient/bryndza"],["Cream","ingredient/cream"],["Smetana","ingredient/smetana"],["Smetana (dairy product)","ingredient/smetana-dairy-product"],["Cheese","ingredient/cheese"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Paraguay cheese","ingredient/paraguay-cheese"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/6/68/Shandong_Tai%27an_corn_flour.jpg/500px-Shandong_Tai%27an_corn_flour.jpg","w":500,"h":500,"by":"François Nguyen","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shandong_Tai'an_corn_flour.jpg"}},"ingredient/japonica-rice":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/japonica-rice","n":"Japonica rice","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 3 dishes across 2 cuisines","intro":[["Japonica rice (Oryza sativa subsp. japonica), sometimes called sinica rice, is one of the two major domestic types of Asian rice varieties. Japonica rice is extensively cultivated and consumed in East Asia and Italy, whereas in most other regions indica rice is the dominant type of rice. Japonica rice originated from Central China, where it was first domesticated along the Yangtze River basin approximately 9,500 to 6,000 years ago."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-japonica-rice"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japonica_rice"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["Korean cuisine","cuisine/korean-cuisine",[["Bap (rice dish)","dish/bap-rice-dish"],["Hwajeon","dish/hwajeon"]]],["Japanese cuisine","cuisine/japane