/kuchmachi"],["Kvass","dish/kvass"],["Lobiani","dish/lobiani"],["Mchadi","dish/mchadi"],["Mujuretuli","dish/mujuretuli"],["Nadugi","dish/nadugi"],["Pkhali","dish/pkhali"],["Shkmeruli","dish/shkmeruli"],["Tsolikouri","dish/tsolikouri"],["Tvishi (wine)","dish/tvishi-wine"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/2/25/Badrijani_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Badrijani_%28cropped%29.jpg","w":500,"h":330,"by":"salvagekat","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Badrijani_(cropped).jpg"}},"dish/ti-hueh-kue":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/ti-hueh-kue","n":"Ti-hueh-kué","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Taiwan cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Ti-hueh-kué, also known as ti-hueh-ko or pig's blood cake, is a blood pudding served on a stick as street food in Taiwan. It is made with steamed pork blood, sticky rice and then coated in peanut powder and coriander with dipping sauces. Pig's blood cake originated in Fujian and developed afterwards in Taiwan. It is eaten as a snack. It can also be cooked in a hot pot. It is served hot by street vendors who keep it warmed in a wooden box or metal steamer. A description from Seriouseats described it as cross between a rice cake and mochi."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-ti-hueh-kue"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ti-hueh-ku%C3%A9"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Taiwan cuisine","cuisine/taiwan-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Cooking in Chinglish","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"cookinginchinglish.com","note":"Taiwanese home recipes from Edwina, who grew up in Taiwan.","url":"https://cookinginchinglish.com","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Tiny Urban Kitchen: Taiwanese Recipes","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"tinyurbankitchen.com","note":"Small set of Taiwanese recipes from Jen Che, a Taiwanese American blogger in Hong Kong.","url":"ht